-- Q220: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (C): Under Section 1031, deferred like-kind exchanges require replacement property identification within 45 days and closing within 180 days of the relinquished property transfer. Patel's March 1 transfer sets the 45-day deadline at April 15, making the April 20 identification attempt invalid with no extension or penalty option. Sharma met both deadlines (identified April 10, closed July 15 at 136 days).
Wrong (A): The 45-day identification deadline is absolute and does not permit modifications or extensions after expiration.
Wrong (B): Identification and closing have separate deadlines of 45 and 180 days respectively, not a combined 45-day window.
Correct (D): No penalty option exists for late identification — the exchange simply fails if no timely-identified property is acquired within 180 days.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 220;

-- Q221: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under Section 1031, mortgage relief is treated as boot received. Evans's $100,000 mortgage relief triggers $100,000 recognized gain (limited by the $200,000 realized gain). If Evans assumed a $60,000 mortgage on the warehouse, liabilities are netted: $100,000 relief minus $60,000 assumed equals $40,000 net boot, reducing recognized gain to $40,000. The netting principle reflects the taxpayer's true economic extraction.
Wrong (B): Mortgage involvement does not disqualify the exchange from Section 1031; it merely triggers partial gain recognition on the boot received.
Wrong (C): Mortgage relief has been treated as boot since the regulations were established under Section 1031.
Correct (D): Liabilities assumed on replacement property directly reduce net boot — this netting is fundamental to Section 1031 calculations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 221;

-- Q223: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under IRC Section 164(b)(6) enacted by the TCJA, the SALT deduction is capped at $10,000 for all filing statuses ($5,000 for MFS). The Nguyens' $20,000 in combined state income and property taxes exceeds the cap by $10,000, losing a significant deduction. The Petersons' $9,000 falls below the cap and is fully deductible. The cap primarily impacts taxpayers in high-tax states.
Correct (B): The $10,000 SALT cap applies to all filing statuses including MFJ, not just single filers.
Wrong (C): The SALT cap was not overturned and remains in effect as enacted by the TCJA.
Wrong (D): The MFJ SALT cap is $10,000 total, not $5,000 per spouse.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 223;

-- Q224: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under IRC Section 162(l), self-employed taxpayers may deduct 100% of health insurance premiums as an above-the-line deduction, limited to net self-employment income. This approximates the exclusion employees receive under Section 106, where employer-paid premiums are excluded from gross income. Walsh's employer-paid $6,000 is excluded from income, and Walsh's share may be pre-tax through a cafeteria plan.
Correct (B): Section 162(l) provides a specific above-the-line deduction for self-employed individuals, so they are not relegated to the 7.5% AGI medical expense floor under Section 213.
Wrong (C): The self-employed health insurance deduction is 100% of premiums (subject to income limits), not 50%.
Wrong (D): The TCJA did not equalize treatment — self-employed and employee health insurance deductions remain structurally different.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 224;

-- Q225: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under Section 170, long-term capital gain property (held more than one year) donated to a public charity is deductible at FMV, subject to a 30% AGI limitation. Park's 3-year holding qualifies for the $20,000 FMV deduction. Kim's 8-month holding makes the stock short-term property, reducing the deduction to basis ($5,000) to prevent claiming deductions on unrealized short-term gains.
Wrong (B): The holding period directly determines whether FMV or basis applies — short-term property must be reduced to basis.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment; longer holding periods enable the larger FMV deduction, not smaller.
Correct (D): The deduction is either FMV or basis depending on holding period, not the gain amount (FMV minus basis).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 225;

-- Q226: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under Section 199A, the QBI deduction is 20% for pass-through income, but SSTBs face a phase-out beginning at $364,200 for MFJ (complete at $464,200). At $400,000 taxable income, Porter (architect, an SSTB) is in the phase-out range and receives a reduced deduction. Martinez's manufacturing business is not an SSTB, so the service-business phase-out does not apply.
Wrong (B): The SSTB classification creates different treatment at higher income levels — Section 199A does not treat all pass-through businesses identically.
Correct (C): SSTBs are not completely excluded; they receive the full deduction below the threshold and a reduced deduction in the phase-out range.
Wrong (D): Manufacturing is a qualifying trade for Section 199A and does generate QBI.$EXPL$
