-- Q229: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under IRC Section 24(a), the child tax credit is $2,000 per qualifying child under 17. The phase-out under Section 24(b) begins at $400,000 AGI for MFJ, reducing the credit by $50 per $1,000 above the threshold. The Andersons at exactly $400,000 receive the full $6,000. The Garcias' $50,000 excess reduces their credit by $2,500, leaving $3,500.
Wrong (B): The phase-out begins at $400,000 for MFJ filers, not $500,000.
Correct (C): The phase-out is gradual, not a cliff — the Andersons at $400,000 receive the full credit since they are at the threshold, not above it.
Wrong (D): Higher AGI reduces the credit through the phase-out mechanism; it does not increase the refundable portion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 229;

-- Q230: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under Section 311, when a corporation distributes appreciated property, it recognizes gain as if sold at FMV. Nexus recognizes $30,000 gain on Park's distribution ($80,000 FMV minus $50,000 basis). However, Section 311(a) prohibits loss recognition on depreciated property distributions, so Nexus cannot deduct the $15,000 loss on Lee's vehicle. Distribution amounts are measured at FMV.
Correct (B): Distributions are measured at FMV, not adjusted basis — FMV represents the shareholder's actual economic benefit received.
Wrong (C): Loss recognition is specifically disallowed on distributions, creating asymmetric treatment between appreciated and depreciated property.
Wrong (D): Gain is recognized on appreciated property distributions under Section 311(b); only losses are disallowed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 230;

-- Q236: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): IRC Section 1378 requires S corporations to use a calendar year unless the corporation establishes a valid business purpose or elects under Section 444 with a required payment. The anti-deferral rationale: individual shareholders file on a calendar year, so a September 30 year-end would defer October through December income up to 15 months.
Correct (B): Fiscal years create real timing mismatches — pass-through rules do not automatically match income to the shareholder's tax year.
Wrong (C): There is no September 30 requirement; estimated tax payments are not the basis for the required year-end rule.
Wrong (D): S corporations default to a calendar year under Section 1378, not the majority shareholder's year.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 236;

-- Q240: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under Section 707(c), a guaranteed payment is determined "without regard to the income of the partnership." Chen's fixed $120,000 qualifies — paid regardless of profits. Webb's 30% of net income varies with partnership income, making it a distributive share. Both are ordinary income subject to self-employment tax, but the guaranteed payment is deducted before allocating remaining income to partners.
Correct (B): The classification test is whether the payment varies with income, not whether it compensates services — Webb's income-contingent payment is a distributive share, not a guaranteed payment.
Wrong (C): This reverses the classification — fixed payments (Chen) are guaranteed payments, not distributions from profits.
Wrong (D): Guaranteed payments under Section 707(c) apply to both services and capital contributions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 240;

-- Q488: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Section 6501(e) extends the assessment period to six years when a taxpayer omits gross income exceeding 25% of the amount stated on the return. Griffin reported $200,000 and omitted $60,000 (30% of reported income), triggering the extended period. Wells's filing status error is not an income omission and does not trigger the six-year rule. Congress designed this extension because unreported income is inherently harder for the IRS to detect.
Correct (B): Only specific triggers extend the statute — income omission exceeding 25%, fraud, or non-filing — not any error on a return.
Wrong (C): The threshold is 25%, not 50%; Griffin's $60,000 omission is 30% of the $200,000 reported and exceeds the threshold.
Wrong (D): Filing status errors do not trigger the extended statute — only substantial income omissions qualify under Section 6501(e).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 488;

-- Q496: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Under UCC Section 2-601, the buyer may accept the whole, reject the whole, or accept any commercial unit(s) and reject the rest. DataTech can reject the 100 dead-pixel monitors while accepting the 400 functioning units (including those with minor packaging damage), avoiding supply disruption. Under Section 2-714, DataTech can recover damages for nonconforming units.
Correct (A): The buyer is never required to accept all goods — the UCC provides three options including partial acceptance by commercial unit.
Wrong (B): Partial acceptance is explicitly permitted under Section 2-601; the perfect tender rule does not mandate all-or-nothing treatment.
Wrong (C): Inspection does not constitute acceptance — the UCC provides a reasonable time to inspect before acceptance occurs under Section 2-606.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 496;

-- Q513: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Under Section 280F, listed property must exceed 50% business use to qualify for MACRS accelerated depreciation. Thompson's 70% use exceeds the threshold, qualifying for accelerated depreciation on the business portion (subject to luxury auto limits). Valdez's 45% use falls at or below 50%, restricting her to straight-line. If Thompson's use later drops below 50%, she must recapture excess depreciation previously claimed.
Wrong (A): The TCJA did not repeal listed property rules for automobiles — the 50% business-use threshold remains in effect under Section 280F.
Correct (B): This reverses the treatment — higher business use qualifies for the more favorable accelerated method, not lower use.
Wrong (C): The threshold is greater than 50%, not 80%, and both taxpayers can claim depreciation on their respective business-use portions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 513;

-- Q514: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under IRC Sec. 168(k), the TCJA set 100% bonus depreciation through 2022, with a 20-percentage-point annual phase-down: 80% (2023), 60% (2024), 40% (2025), 20% (2026). Meridian's 2025 purchase qualifies for 40% ($200,000) while Atlas's 2026 purchase qualifies for 20% ($100,000), illustrating the declining first-year benefit.
Correct (B): The TCJA did not make 100% bonus depreciation permanent — it phases down 20 percentage points annually beginning in 2023.
Wrong (C): Bonus rates decrease over time, not increase — the phase-down does not encourage delayed investment.
Wrong (D): The phase-down uses 20-percentage-point annual reductions from 100%, not 10% increments from 60%.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 514;

-- Q515: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under IRC Section 168(c) as amended by the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1993, nonresidential real property placed in service after May 13, 1993 uses a 39-year recovery period, while property placed in service on or before that date retains the 31.5-year period. Morrison''s April warehouse qualifies for 31.5 years; the June warehouse uses 39 years.
Wrong (B): The change was not retroactive — property placed in service before May 13, 1993 retained the 31.5-year period already in effect.
Correct (C): The 27.5-year recovery period applies only to residential rental property under Section 168(c), not nonresidential commercial warehouses.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement to use ADS for pre-threshold property; both warehouses use GDS with their respective recovery periods.$EXPL$
