-- Q538: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (B): Under Section 465, a taxpayer''s deductible loss is limited to the amount at risk, which includes cash invested and recourse debt but excludes nonrecourse debt where the taxpayer bears no personal economic risk. Webber''s at-risk amount is $80,000 (cash only), so only $80,000 of the $150,000 loss is deductible. The remaining $70,000 is suspended until additional at-risk basis is created.
Correct (A): The at-risk rules apply to all taxpayers regardless of AGI level; there is no $250,000 income threshold.
Wrong (C): Cash is included in the at-risk amount while nonrecourse debt is excluded — this choice reverses the inclusion rule.
Wrong (D): The entire purpose of Section 465 is to distinguish recourse from nonrecourse debt; nonrecourse borrowings are excluded from the at-risk amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 538;

-- Q539: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (C): Under Section 469, passive losses can only offset passive income. Torres has no passive income, so the entire $45,000 limited partnership loss is suspended and carried forward until she generates passive income or fully disposes of the activity in a taxable transaction. The compartmentalization prevents taxpayers from using tax shelter losses to reduce tax on wages and portfolio income.
Wrong (A): Wages are active income, which is precisely the category that passive losses cannot offset under Section 469.
Wrong (B): Dividends are portfolio income, not passive income — Section 469(e) explicitly excludes portfolio income from the passive category.
Correct (D): The TCJA did not repeal or modify the passive activity loss rules under Section 469; they remain fully in effect.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 539;

-- Q545: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Under Section 469, passive losses offset only passive income in the current year. Morrison''s $30,000 passive income from Activity A absorbs $30,000 of the $50,000 passive loss, leaving $20,000 suspended — wages cannot absorb the excess. Upon a complete taxable disposition of Activity B, all suspended losses are released and deductible against any income type including wages.
Wrong (A): Passive losses cannot offset wages or other nonpassive income in the current year under Section 469, regardless of income level.
Correct (B): Having passive income is beneficial, not disqualifying — it provides a source to absorb passive losses, allowing $30,000 of the loss here.
Wrong (C): The $25,000 rental allowance under Section 469(i) applies only to active participation in rental real estate, not to general passive activities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 545;

-- Q551: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under Section 469(g), a complete disposition in a fully taxable transaction releases all accumulated suspended passive losses, deductible against any income type. Kowalski''s $120,000 in suspended losses would be fully deductible — netting against the $30,000 gain and producing a $90,000 net deduction against other income. In a gift, Section 469(j)(6) adds suspended losses to the donee''s basis instead of releasing them to the donor.
Wrong (B): The complete disposition rule under Section 469(g) specifically allows deduction of all suspended losses upon a taxable sale.
Wrong (C): The full $120,000 is released in a taxable sale, not limited to the $30,000 gain amount; the excess offsets other income.
Correct (D): Gifts and nontaxable transfers do not trigger loss release — only fully taxable dispositions to unrelated parties qualify under Section 469(g).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 551;

-- Q825: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Cross-site scripting allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users, enabling session hijacking, credential harvesting, and content manipulation. As classified in OWASP Top 10 A03:2021 and aligned with NIST SP 800-53 Section SI-10 (Information Input Validation), the injected script executes in the victim''s browser context with their session privileges.
Correct (B): XSS targets the client-side browser and cannot directly corrupt backend database records; database corruption would require server-side attacks such as SQL injection.
Wrong (C): XSS does not cause denial-of-service conditions on the web server; it operates within the victim''s browser session rather than overwhelming server resources.
Wrong (D): XSS does not expose application source code; it injects scripts into rendered pages but does not provide access to server-side code repositories or files.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 825;

-- Q830: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Multi-factor authentication combining a password (something you know) with a one-time code from an authenticator app (something you have) provides the strongest protection against credential-based attacks. Under NIST SP 800-63B Section 4, compromising one factor alone is insufficient for unauthorized access.
Correct (B): Username and password represents a single authentication factor and remains vulnerable to phishing and credential-stuffing attacks that compromised the accounts initially.
Wrong (C): Security questions are a second knowledge factor, not a distinct factor type, and answers are often guessable or discoverable through social media research.
Wrong (D): A four-digit PIN provides only 10,000 possible combinations and represents a weak single knowledge factor easily defeated by brute force attacks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 830;

-- Q882: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Best practice under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320) is to obtain a SOC 2 Type II report annually, covering a continuous 12-month examination period that aligns with the prior report''s end date to avoid coverage gaps. This provides user entity auditors with current, uninterrupted evidence of control operating effectiveness.
Wrong (A): A five-year cycle would leave user entities without current assurance for unacceptably long periods, undermining the purpose of continuous monitoring.
Correct (B): Monthly reporting is impractical and unnecessary; annual coverage with no gaps between periods satisfies assurance requirements efficiently.
Wrong (C): Obtaining reports only on request creates unpredictable gaps in coverage and does not provide the continuous assurance user entities need.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 882;

