-- Q2512: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Under RULLCA Section 304, LLC members receive limited liability protection while retaining the right to actively participate in management. This is the key structural advantage over a general partnership, where active management participation exposes partners to unlimited personal liability.
Correct (A): This statement is false. LLC members can participate in management under RULLCA without losing their liability protection, unlike limited partners under RULPA.
Wrong (B): Most states allow single-member LLCs. There is no requirement that an LLC have at least two members under RULLCA.
Wrong (C): LLCs are not always taxed as corporations. By default, a multi-member LLC is taxed as a partnership, and a single-member LLC is disregarded for federal tax purposes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2512;

-- Q2514: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Under RULLCA Section 503, a creditor of an individual LLC member is limited to obtaining a charging order against the member''s transferable interest. The charging order entitles the creditor to receive distributions that would otherwise go to the debtor-member but does not allow seizure of assets or forced dissolution.
Correct (A): A personal judgment creditor of a member cannot collect directly from LLC assets. The LLC is a separate legal entity, and its assets are not subject to a member''s personal debts.
Wrong (B): The creditor cannot seize and sell the membership interest itself. The exclusive remedy under most statutes is a charging order on distributions, not a forced sale.
Wrong (C): A personal creditor of one member cannot compel dissolution of the entire LLC. The charging order protects the other members from disruption caused by one member''s debts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2514;

-- Q2821: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (D): Under IRC Section 1362(d)(3), if an S corporation with accumulated C corporation E&P has passive investment income exceeding 25% of gross receipts for three consecutive years, the S election is automatically terminated on the first day of the fourth year. The corporation reverts to C corporation status.
Correct (A): The IRC does not impose a $10,000 fine for excess passive investment income; the consequence is termination of S status under Section 1362(d)(3).
Wrong (B): Section 1375 imposes an entity-level tax on excess net passive income, but this is not a flat 20% penalty and is separate from the termination rule.
Wrong (C): The consequence falls on the corporation''s S election status, not as an additional tax on shareholders individually.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2821;

-- Q13241: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Price-to-book is the standard valuation multiple for banks and financial institutions under ASC 820-10-35. Bank balance sheets consist primarily of financial assets and liabilities carried at or near fair value, making book value a meaningful proxy for net asset value.
Wrong (B): EV/Revenue is poorly suited to banks because interest income and interest expense are both operating items, making net interest income rather than gross revenue the relevant metric.
Correct (C): P/E can be used for banks, but earnings volatility from credit losses and interest rate swings makes P/B a more stable benchmark.
Wrong (D): EV/EBITDA is inappropriate for banks because interest expense is an operating cost, not a financing cost, and depreciation is minimal.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13241;

-- Q13245: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under DCF valuation principles consistent with ASC 820, the terminal value formula assumes cash flows have reached a sustainable, steady-state growth pattern. A biotech company reaching steady-state margins in Year 8 requires a longer explicit forecast so the terminal value reflects normalized cash flows rather than rapidly changing margins.
Wrong (B): A longer forecast period does not always improve accuracy; it introduces more estimation uncertainty for companies already at steady state.
Correct (C): There is no minimum number of years required for the terminal value formula to be valid.
Wrong (D): Longer projection periods do not reduce the discount rate''s influence; they shift more value into the explicit period but the rate still compounds over all years.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13245;

-- Q14041: Fix #6
